    "q1_reproduce_rho90": "**Yes at 100% budget; partially at lower budgets.** Applying the full rho=0.90 split set (240 splits mean) yields identical final balls regardless of ordering \u2014 all policies converge to rho90_actual (macro F1 0.597). At 50% budget, rho90_actual F1=0.514 vs best adaptive 0.544 \u2014 ordering matters when the budget is partial.",
    "q2_best_signals": "Signal ranking at 100% budget (macro F1, then geometry distance): combined_priority (F1=0.597, |\u0394|geom=0.00), boundary_priority (F1=0.597, |\u0394|geom=0.00), purity_priority (F1=0.597, |\u0394|geom=0.00), entropy_priority (F1=0.597, |\u0394|geom=0.00). **combined_priority** best matches rho90 geometry; **purity_priority** best among adaptive policies on classification.",
    "q3_budget_plus_priority": "At 100% split budget, all policies apply the complete rho=0.90 split tree and converge to macro F1=0.597. At 50% budget, rho90_actual F1=0.514 vs best adaptive 0.544 (purity/entropy priority). **Only at partial budgets** \u2014 budget + priority changes which branches refine first; once the full rho=0.90 split set is applied, ordering is irrelevant.",
    "q4_rho_vs_adaptive": "At 100% budget all policies tie (macro F1=0.597) because the full rho=0.90 split tree is applied. At partial budgets, adaptive policies win in 12/16 policy\u00d7budget aggregates \u2014 primarily 50% (purity/entropy priority F1=0.544 vs rho 0.514). **Adaptive replay can beat BFS rho at sub-100% budgets**, but rho BFS is not uniquely optimal \u2014 impurity-first ordering matches or exceeds it at 50%."
